In financial econometrics, evaluating the performance of a portfolio often requires dynamic execution of mathematical models. The following R script demonstrates how to construct a formula dynamically, evaluate it, and drop insignificant variables from a linear regression model.

# Load necessary libraries
library(quantmod)
library(PerformanceAnalytics)

# Define the function to evaluate model fit
evaluate_model <- function(data, dependent_var, independent_vars) {
  # Construct formula string
  formula_str <- paste(dependent_var, "~", paste(independent_vars, collapse = " + "))

  # Execute the linear model
  model <- eval(parse(text = paste("lm(", formula_str, ", data = data)")))

  # Summary of the evaluation
  model_summary <- summary(model)

  # Drop variables with p-value > 0.05
  p_values <- model_summary$coefficients[, 4]
  significant_vars <- names(p_values[p_values < 0.05])

  # Remove intercept from the list of variables to keep
  significant_vars <- significant_vars[significant_vars != "(Intercept)"]

  if (length(significant_vars) < length(independent_vars)) {
    print("Dropping insignificant variables and re-executing model.")
    return(evaluate_model(data, dependent_var, significant_vars))
  } else {
    return(model)
  }
}

This recursive evaluation ensures that we only retain factors that have statistically significant predictive power over the asset's returns. Students should be careful when using 'eval' and 'parse' as they can lead to runtime inefficiencies or obscure debugging errors if the independent_vars list contains unexpected formatting. Ensure all data objects are properly structured before calling this function.

