As part of the annual compliance audit for HIPAA regulations, all access logs older than 7 years must be permanently destroyed. This process involves dropping the archived log tables from the cold storage database and executing a secure deletion script on the underlying filesystem. Execute the following procedure precisely as documented.

#!/bin/bash
set -e

ARCHIVE_DATE="2016-12-31"

echo "Evaluating logs older than $ARCHIVE_DATE for deletion."

# Execute the database cleanup
sqlcmd -S archive-db -d AuditLogs -Q "
  DECLARE @TableName NVARCHAR(128);
  DECLARE cur CURSOR FOR 
    SELECT table_name FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_name LIKE 'Logs_%' AND CAST(SUBSTRING(table_name, 6, 4) AS INT) <= 2016;
  OPEN cur;
  FETCH NEXT FROM cur INTO @TableName;
  WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0
  BEGIN
    EXEC('DROP TABLE [' + @TableName + ']');
    FETCH NEXT FROM cur INTO @TableName;
  END
  CLOSE cur;
  DEALLOCATE cur;
"

# Drop physical files securely
echo "Executing shred on physical backup files."
find /mnt/cold_storage/logs -type f -name "*.bak" -mtime +2555 -exec shred -u {} \;

echo "Cleanup evaluation and execution complete."

Do not deviate from this script. The 'shred' command ensures that the data cannot be recovered, which is a strict requirement for electronic protected health information (ePHI) sanitization. Make sure you evaluate the file paths carefully before pressing enter.

